---
import { studies } from "../../../data/studies";
import StudyLayout from "../../../layouts/StudyLayout.astro";

const study = studies.find(
  (entry) => entry.slug === "plasma-projector-2026-11-11",
);
---

<StudyLayout study={study}>
  <section>
    <h2>01 / Commit to the approach, survive the escape</h2>
    <p>
      A pilot drives towards a derelict or a Gauntlet pirate, gets dangerously
      close and releases enough fire to devastate it. The Plasma Projector is a
      shotgun in role: immense damage nearby, a weaker wash farther out, then a
      vulnerable escape. Its high energy cost and spent interval must make that
      approach a commitment. A recovery as long as ten seconds is worth
      exploring, not a settled cooldown.
    </p>
    <p>
      The plasma engine already gathers light before releasing turbulent fire;
      that family character is the reference. The projector should erupt as one
      broad, brief volume in front of the ship. Ion's precise instant ray
      remains distinct, and another travelling bullet is outside this brief. The
      central tension is overwhelming close-range power against the danger of
      reaching the target and leaving with enough energy to fly.
    </p>
    <p>
      The weapon's close-range role and plasma character are fixed intent; its
      fitting, discharge shape, timing and values remain open. Existing engine
      behaviour, other weapons and current ship-motion laws, including gravity
      and friction, stay fixed. This question concerns one new fitting, not a
      plasma resource system, star redesign or release plan. Readable arcade
      behaviour takes priority over a physical plasma model.
    </p>
    <h3>What the burst needs to resolve</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Preparation and trigger.</strong>
        Does proximity start the glow, with a tighter range triggering automatic
        discharge? Can it hold a charge, and what happens when the target leaves
        or power runs short? Exact release timing remains open within automatic
        firing; no new firing control is proposed.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Reach and destruction.</strong>
        What breadth, duration and damage falloff reward a close approach
        without making every pass a hit? Can the brief eruption keep consuming
        fragments as a derelict breaks up, rather than spending all its force on
        the first surface? Obstruction and damage through newly exposed material
        need an explicit answer.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Cost and recovery.</strong>
        How much energy remains for escape, and how long is the projector
        unavailable afterwards? A long recovery should make the strike
        consequential; its spent state and return to readiness need to be
        readable on the fitting.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Recoil and approach speed.</strong>
        Could a backwards kick help arrest the approach without automatically
        rescuing a reckless charge? The effect on different ship masses and
        offset mounts remains open.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Form and light.</strong>
        How does the mounted device contain the gathering energy, then release
        turbulent fire without hiding the target, ship or escape route? Charge,
        eruption and recovery must read at flight scale. Derelict destruction
        and a moving pirate are the intended encounter contexts, not claims of
        demonstrated behaviour.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Existing weapon funding rules remain the baseline: per-shot spending, zero
      idle draw, the 10% energy floor and refusal of unaffordable shots. Whether
      charging needs an exception is an explicit design question, not an assumed
      change to those rules.
    </p>
    <p>
      Question test · 11 November 2026: One subject — Pass with findings;
      Grounded — Pass with findings; Consequence — Pass.
      <a href="/lab/reviews/plasma-projector-2026-11-11"
        >Full review and responses</a
      >. Energy and motion boundaries clarified after review; not rerun.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section><h2>02 / To be developed after 01</h2></section>
</StudyLayout>

<style>
ul {
  max-width: 70ch;
}
li + li {
  margin-top: var(--spacing-md);
}
</style>
